m or support your local  library next an absolute classic 60 G  pieces by Richard Feynman my copy is  extremely worn I have read this several  times and I've taken it on quite a few  holidays with me Feynman was known as  the Great Communicator quite apart from  being one of the most brilliant  physicists of the 20th century and in a  book of six chapters which are based on  lectures he gave it's it's basically  extract and the fireman lectures on  physics and they cover basic physics  like atoms and you know the notion of  how the physical world is constructed  and goes through gravitation  conservation of energy and some quantum  mechanics in the last chapter it's  basically like dipping your toe into  different bits of physics it's very very  accessible I'm not the world  biggest fan of Fineman even I have to  concede that it's it's you know  phenomenally well done it's not a  textbook to learn from but it's a very  very good place to start and if you're  interested if you think you might be  interested in physics in university I  would recommend this book probably above  any other also there's a sequel six not  so easy pieces which is about special  and general relativity if you know  you're interested in physics then check  this out they would recommend this is  quite a bit harder than six easy pieces  as a title suggests it was recommended  reading as part of my general relativity  course Oxford it's it can be a bit tough  going but it's it's absolutely  fascinating stuff and again very well  explained next not so much a physics  books as a maths book  Alex this adventures in numberland by  Alex bellos this is a book which  completely changed my view on maths when  I was in school I I never really liked  math I viewed it very much as a toward  that you used to do other things like  physics but what this book did was it  shows the the side of maths that you  just don't learn in school it shows the  more cultural side of things again the  historical development of the subject  and then things like how mass controls  the global gambling industry how PI as a  number has evolved over time and you  know ideas about different kinds of  infinities fans of John Green should  definitely read this one but it's  definitely a book which I recommend to  anybody who scientifically minded you  know in particular if you thinking of  doing physics in university you need to  get used to the idea of using math as a  tool but also appreciating maths more  broadly and this but does that  fantastically well this is something  which I I would recommend to literally  everybody now I'm going along two books  together the first of which is in search  of Schrodinger's cat by John Griffin and  the second one is the elegant universe  by Brian Greene now the reason that not  these two books together is because they  are books which I've read a long time  ago these were two books which really  got me into physics and kind of helped  me decide that I wanted to do physics in  university now because of this I can't  really comment on how good they were  because I can't remember a lot of the  discourse I couldn't I can criticize it  for example but I know that they got me  interested they're two very  well-respected books in such  Schrodinger's cat  is about quantum theory and a journalist  so not a scientist perspective they're  journalists perspective on quantum  mechanics and trying to get to the  bottom of what people mean when they  talk about these Schrodinger's cat a  thought experiment whilst the other  commune of us is about string theory and  Brian Greene is one of the top  scientists in the field he's written  this book about string theory which  isn't just about string theory it also  talks about all the science that's  necessary to understand in order to get  to string theory which is one of the  pinnacles of non physics and you know  it's a very good account of a lot of  modern physics I should say with a  caveat this is a little bit dense like  it's the kind of book you have to read a  page once or twice and then move on  there's a version which is not quite so  dense and a little more accessible  called the fabric of the cosmos also by  Brian Greene but this is the book I  think really we should maybe decide I  wanted to do a physics degree so take  from that what you will  lastly for the popular science books  quite an unusual one which is the  strangest man my grandfather now this is  a scientific biography and it's the best  biography I've ever read the titular  strangest man is Paul Dirac who is one  of my scientific heroes and has been  called the great expressionist physicist  since Newton now some people will argue  with that but it's undoubted undoubtedly  he was one of the greatest physicists of  all time he also had a fascinating  private life though notably he never  really talked people coined the Dirac as  a unit which was one word per hour and  they'd use it to measure how many words  it was necessary to keep a conversation  with him going he also had a very tragic  relationship with his father which is  times frustrating to meet about a lot of  times very touching and sad his father  in his child in Derek's childhood really  set him up as who he was for better and  for worse and I'll leave you to read  more about that in the book but this is  a very very very good book site so it's  references it also explains his science  which is very very high-level but it  does so in an accessible way ok so  that's it I hope you found this video  useful if you have any book  recommendations of your own please feel  free to leave your suggestions in the  comments and if you have any suggestions  videos you'd like to see me do in the  future also leave them in the comments  lastly my friend Fran this is Oxford  University physics Society president has  just set up her own YouTube channel  doing videos about different kinds of  physics she's just uploaded her first  which is about Cherenkov radiation and  if you'd like to check that out I  recommend you click her face on the  screen wherever I've put it and maybe  subscribe to it thanks for watching and  I will see you again next time

